---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import CardGrid from '../components/CardGrid.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What UniDPP does"
  description="The capabilities that define the UniDPP international framework: multi-jurisdiction profiles, offline verification, event-sourced lifecycle, graded trust, and open semantics."
  eyebrow="Reference — capabilities"
>
  <h1>What UniDPP is built to do</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    UniDPP is an international framework and open reference implementation. The
    capabilities below are the ones the architecture is designed around — each
    is implemented in the open repositories and exercised by the end-to-end demo.
  </p>

  <section id="capabilities" class="plate" aria-labelledby="cap-h">
    <h2 id="cap-h">Capability map</h2>
    <CardGrid>
      <Card title="One identity, many legal views">
        <p>
          A product carries one identity and one append-only record. Jurisdiction,
          sector, and characteristic <em>profiles</em> are registered lenses on that
          record — each independently versioned, signed, and verified under its own
          trust anchors. Adding a market is a dated binding, not a second passport.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Offline minimum (Tier A)">
        <p>
          A fixed carrier subset — identity, status, critical flags, multi-suite
          signatures — verifies with cached anchors when origins are unreachable.
          Verdicts name three readings and state coverage explicitly; staleness
          never silently passes.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Event-sourced lifecycle">
        <p>
          Custody, repair, software update, modification, recall, and end-of-life
          are typed events. Illegal status transitions are rejected. History is
          never rewritten; as-of queries reconstruct legal state at any time T.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Transformation algebra">
        <p>
          Split, combine, and derive passports with mass-balance accounting and
          taint propagation through the provenance graph. End-of-waste is a new
          issuance. Recycled-content claims recompute from the graph.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Graded trust (SIGNATIF)">
        <p>
          Multi-suite co-signatures (including sovereign suites), threshold roots,
          transparency-log anchoring, and revocation whose <em>reason</em> decides
          retroactivity. Verifiers fetch live trust lists and resolve under their
          own acceptance policy.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Semantic registry (FERIN / 19135)">
        <p>
          Data elements, profiles, protocol bindings, crypto suites, and units are
          governed register items — discoverable, version-pinned, supersedable.
          The EN 18223 §4.3 vacancy is filled by a federated discovery layer, not a
          single dictionary host.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Edge and silent objects">
        <p>
          Capability classes S0–S3: silent products are fully servable with zero
          device participation; instrumented products commit log prefixes and
          reveal later. Freshness is a displayable verdict, profile-gated.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Open stack, open evidence">
        <p>
          Libraries, services, conformance fixtures (including negative cases),
          terminology dataset, and the E8 end-to-end demo are public. The EU EN
          series is one jurisdiction profile of the same core — not the envelope.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </CardGrid>
  </section>

  <section id="alongside" class="plate" aria-labelledby="along-h">
    <h2 id="along-h">Alongside regional implementations</h2>
    <p>
      Regional and sector projects implement the rules of a particular regime.
      UniDPP implements the <em>mechanism</em> by which many regimes share one
      product identity. The relationship is complementary:
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        <strong>EN 18216–18246 / freeDPP.</strong> The published European series
        and its open server are the natural content of an <em>EU profile</em> on
        the UniDPP core. A UniDPP deployment can serve EN 18222-shaped requests as
        one render among others.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenDPP and AAS-oriented platforms.</strong> Where a deployment
        already uses Asset Administration Shell tooling, UniDPP keeps AAS as an
        informative mapping — the core model stays representation-neutral.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>National carrier and cert layers</strong> (for example GB/T 33993,
        CQC digital certificates). These become protocol bindings and certificate
        subregister joins in the discovery registry, not competing roots.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="try" class="plate" aria-labelledby="try-h">
    <h2 id="try-h">See it run</h2>
    <p>
      The public end-to-end walks one product — a Japanese e-bike with EU and CN
      parts — through assembly, EU placement, offline border verification,
      firmware derestriction, repair, auction, recall, and end-of-life.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/implementation/demos/">Demos</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/unidpp/unidpp-e2e">unidpp-e2e on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/framework/">Framework</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .plain {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
    max-width: var(--prose);
  }
  .plain li {
    margin: 0.65rem 0;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
  .plain strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
